Revision history for MCP-Hub

{{$NEXT}}
  - Initial release. A single HTTP MCP server (`mcp-hub`) that embeds any
    number of stdio MCP servers, in-process Perl MCP servers, and remote
    HTTP MCP servers (Streamable HTTP and HTTP+SSE), exposes each of them to
    many agents on the machine as its own endpoint, starts them lazily on
    first use and stops them when idle, decides per client which of them it
    may use, and prints ready-to-paste client configuration. A lot of MCP
    for very little RAM.
  - Configuration in JSON or YAML, chosen by file extension; one data
    model, the same validation and error messages for both. `${VAR}` and
    `${VAR:-default}` expand in server entries and in `hub.listen`,
    `hub.cache_dir` and client tokens, so no secret has to sit in the file.
  - Live config reload that touches only what changed: `mcp-hub reload`,
    `SIGHUP`, `POST /_hub/reload`, or `hub.auto_reload` to watch the file.
    Unchanged servers keep their process; profile and tool-filter edits
    apply in place; an invalid file is refused as a whole and the running
    config stays in effect.
  - Web setup page at `GET /`: ready-to-paste `.mcp.json`, plus each
    server's tools and instructions; token-gated in clients mode, the token
    accepted from the `Authorization` header only.
  - A server that cannot be built, that crash-loops, or (for a remote HTTP
    upstream) that repeatedly fails to connect stays visible as `failed`
    with its reason (`mcp-hub status`, `hub_status`, setup page, 503 body)
    instead of disappearing; `mcp-hub refresh NAME` retries it. A stdio
    server whose command cannot be executed says so in its reason ("is it
    installed and executable?"). The stdio crash-loop window (three exits
    within five seconds) is the upstream's own `fail_window` attribute,
    apart from the SIGKILL grace period.
    A stdio server that closes its stdout but keeps running is killed after
    five seconds.
  - `mcp-hub status`, `refresh` and `reload` take `--url` to reach a hub that
    does not listen where the config says, and `--token` (or `$MCP_HUB_TOKEN`)
    to authenticate with a token given on the spot. Given both, the command
    never reads the config file, so a broken config no longer stops a command
    that was told exactly where the daemon is and how to authenticate.
  - Native in-process Perl servers, no subprocess per session:
    ClaudeHistory, ClaudeSessions and Status.
  - Docker image (`raudssus/mcp-hub`), batteries-included (Node, Python,
    Deno, Bun), with a bundled `docker-compose.yml`. It finds its config as
    `mcp.json`, `mcp.yml` or `mcp.yaml` under the mounted `/config`
    (first that exists, JSON leading), so a YAML config is picked up without
    an env override while the extension still chooses the parser.
  - A JSON-RPC error that is not an object -- a plain string or a list -- is
    reported with its message instead of leaving the request pending forever.
  - An HTTP+SSE upstream whose `endpoint` event names a URL on another origin
    is refused: the connect fails with that reason, and the foreign host
    receives neither the request nor the configured headers.
  - An exit is counted once, whether the child died during the handshake or
    later: a stdio server that dies at startup gets three attempts, not two.
  - Docker upstreams: a `docker` entry mounts an MCP server by image name,
    a `compose` entry one service of a compose project, both started lazily
    and stopped when idle. The mode is detected: a `port`, or one EXPOSEd or
    published port, means HTTP; none means stdio; `"stdio": true` forces
    stdio. Containers are named and labelled per hub; leftovers of a crashed
    hub are removed by those labels only.
  - A compose entry starts its service plus any dependencies that are not
    running yet, stops only that service, and never stops, recreates or
    restarts anything that already exists; what the service waits for in
    `depends_on` is waited for before the handshake. Compose entries need
    Docker Compose 2 or later; the Docker image ships the compose plugin.
  - `hub.docker_command` picks the container CLI (Podman works) and
    `hub.docker_network` the network HTTP containers join.
  - `mcp-hub daemon` waits up to 20 seconds on shutdown until the containers
    it started are gone.
  - A JSON `true` is no longer accepted where an integer is expected, such as
    `idle_timeout` or `port`.
  - Build tooling for a standalone `mcp-hub` binary (PAR::Packer) for
    machines without Perl: `scripts/build-binary.sh` packs every module the
    hub loads by name, `scripts/verify-binary.sh` checks it against the Perl
    CLI, `scripts/smoke-binary.sh` runs a real daemon without Perl. The
    binary speaks TLS with its own OpenSSL 3.5, linked statically by
    `scripts/build-binary-openssl.sh`, and trusts the system's CA
    certificates below /etc/ssl, else a packed Mozilla CA bundle
    (`SSL_CERT_FILE`/`SSL_CERT_DIR` override). It has no EV, and `class`
    entries are limited to the bundled native servers (README, "Standalone
    binary"). No prebuilt binaries are published yet.
  - Forked class workers: `"hub": {"fork": true}` on a `class` entry lists
    the class's tools from the hub with no process running and runs its
    calls in a worker forked from the hub (Linux), with lazy start, idle
    stop and the crash-loop rules of a stdio child. `mcp-hub status` shows
    such a worker's PSS. The hub stays one daemon; its only children are
    the upstreams and these workers.
  - A `class` entry may name a class whose instances hold their MCP::Server
    and return it from a `server` method.
  - A `class` entry refuses `env`, `cwd`, `headers` and `type`, and, unless
    forked, the `hub` timeouts and `always_on`, naming the JSON path,
    instead of silently ignoring them.
  - IO::Socket::SSL 2.009 is now a hard requirement, for https `url`
    upstreams and an https `hub.listen`.
  - An https `url` upstream, or a compose entry with an https `url`, on a
    perl without TLS (IO::Socket::SSL missing or older than 2.009, or
    MOJO_NO_TLS set) is now a failed upstream whose reason names the fix,
    instead of answering 503 "not ready yet" forever. The compose entry fails
    when it is built, before any compose command runs, instead of starting
    the service and timing out.
  - `mcp-hub config`, `status`, `refresh` and `reload` no longer carry
    `hub.listen`'s query (`?cert=…&key=…`) into the base URL, so an https
    listen works without `--url`.
  - A `command` entry refuses `headers` and any `type` but `stdio`, and a
    `url` entry refuses `env`, `cwd`, `args` and `hub.idle_timeout`, naming the
    JSON path, instead of silently ignoring them.
  - An upstream whose first manifest fetch fails (at daemon start, as an
    always_on start, or on a refresh) is now `failed` with the reason, shown in
    status and in its 503, instead of answering "not ready yet" for good;
    `mcp-hub refresh` brings it back.
  - The build error of a broken non-Perl entry is logged once, not twice.
  - Standalone binary: `"hub": {"fork": true}` works with the bundled natives
    on Linux; the binary smoke now runs one in a forked worker and checks the
    worker never moves the hub's offsets on the PAR archive.
  - A stdio upstream and every docker/compose CLI run no longer inherit the
    hub's listening socket (or any other fd above stderr): a child that
    outlived a crashed hub kept its port bound.
  - A stdio upstream, a docker/compose CLI run and a forked class worker no
    longer inherit MOJO_REUSE, Mojo's list of the hub's listening sockets by
    fd -- fds the child does not hold. An entry's own env may still set it.
  - mcp-hub refuses prefork, cgi and psgi, named or picked by PLACK_ENV or
    GATEWAY_INTERFACE, saying why and pointing at mcp-hub daemon; the command
    list no longer offers them or Mojolicious' generate/inflate/cpanify.
  - hypnotoad bin/mcp-hub is refused like prefork, cgi and psgi, pointing at
    mcp-hub daemon.
  - The global --config/-c is only read before the command name, so
    get -c JSON and daemon -c N keep their own -c.
  - mcp-hub --config (or -c) without a PATH, or with an empty one, is an error
    instead of silently reading the default file or none.
  - mcp-hub help shows mcp-hub's own usage and examples instead of
    Mojolicious'.
  - /all keeps the title, icons and _meta of every tool and prompt, as the
    server's own endpoint lists them; they were dropped since v1.
  - An HTTP+SSE upstream whose stream the remote drops is stopped and
    reconnects on its next call, also after an earlier stop or failed start;
    it used to stay ready over the dead stream, every call timing out, until
    the daemon was restarted.
  - /all keeps the scopes, cache_ttl and cache_scope of an in-process
    class's tools and prompts. Once scopes are enforced, /all refuses and
    hides what the class's own endpoint refuses instead of serving it; the
    hub enforces none yet, so no call got through. Its lists also carry the
    class's cache ttl and the private cache scope a scoped prompt needs.
  - /all filters prompts by the client's profile: prompts/list no longer
    lists the prompts of servers outside it, and prompts/get answers them as
    not found. Before, any client -- a tokenless one under public_profile
    too -- could list and run every server's prompts on /all, starting a
    stdio server to do it.
  - /all decides what a profile may see by the server each tool and prompt
    came from, not by the part of its name before the first `__`. Before,
    with servers `p` and `p__x`, a client whose profile allows only `p`
    could list and run every tool and prompt of `p__x` on /all, and a
    profile allowing `p__x` saw none of its own. Two servers can still
    produce one /all name (`p`'s `x__echo`, `p__x`'s `echo`): both are
    listed and a call reaches the first.
  - /all lists each tool and prompt with its own title, icons and _meta
    when two servers produce the same name (`p`'s `x__echo`, `p__x`'s
    `echo`). Before, both showed those of the last, so a client whose
    profile allows only `p` saw `p__x`'s.
  - /all answers server/discover per client profile: only the instructions
    of servers the profile allows, not marked publicly cacheable, and the
    tools and prompts capabilities (in the legacy initialize too) only when
    the profile leaves any. Before, any client of /all -- a tokenless one
    under public_profile too -- got every server's instructions under
    `## <name>`, naming servers its profile hides.
  - A reload that adds, removes or changes a server, or changes the access
    rules, also sends notifications/prompts/list_changed to /all
    listeners, not only tools/list_changed.
  - A reload frees the upstreams it removes or replaces. A tool, prompt or
    resource held its upstream for the daemon's lifetime, so every removed
    or replaced stdio, http, sse, docker, compose or fork upstream stayed in
    memory; a call to a primitive whose upstream is gone now answers with
    an error naming it.
  - An HTTP+SSE upstream stopped between two requests of its handshake no
    longer leaves the next request waiting for request_timeout (forever with
    request_timeout 0) and the start stuck with it; a request with no
    stream to answer on fails at once.
  - A stop during the start of a Streamable HTTP upstream calls the start
    off: the request in flight is cut off, the upstream stays stopped
    instead of going ready, and nothing is logged or counted as a failure.
    The same holds for either HTTP transport when the stop lands during a
    prompts/list or resources/list. A call in flight on a ready upstream is
    still left to finish.
  - search_conversations tests an ASCII search word against the raw JSON line
    and decodes only lines that can match (a rare word over 500 MB: 4.1 s ->
    1.7 s; 114 s -> 1.8 s with pure-Perl JSON).
  - list_running_sessions reads only the head and tail of each session file,
    so its cost no longer grows with the session (8 x 73 MB: 7 ms instead of
    4.6 s).
  - ClaudeHistory reads session files line by line and, for a file that only
    grew, only what was appended; a cold search reads each file once. Peak
    memory no longer follows the largest file (59 MB instead of 266 MB for
    8 x 73 MB).
  - A Streamable HTTP upstream opens a new session on every start, its
    initialize no longer carrying the id of the session before. A stop ends
    the old session with a DELETE once no call is left on it, and a remote
    that refuses (405) or cannot be reached is fine.
  - A start of an HTTP upstream asked for right after a stop is now a fresh
    start, begun once the stopped start has settled, instead of failing as
    stopped along with it.
  - A session-file line that is JSON but not an object (`[1]`, `42`, `"x"`)
    is now skipped like an unreadable one; before, it made list_projects,
    list_sessions, search_conversations, get_conversation and
    list_running_sessions fail for every session.
  - get_conversation reads the session's own file (`<session_id>.jsonl`, as
    Claude Code names it) first, so on a cold cache it no longer reads every
    session file; a session in a file named otherwise is still found.
  - A reload that removes a ClaudeHistory, ClaudeSessions or Status entry no
    longer keeps its instance alive through the instance's own tools (an
    instance served on its own endpoint is still held by its transport).
  - A Streamable HTTP upstream whose remote has ended its session (a 404 on
    a request carrying the session id) opens a new session and sends the
    request once more on it, instead of failing every call on the dead
    session until a reload; calls that lose the same session share one new
    session, and no DELETE goes out for a session the remote ended.
  - Streamable HTTP error results no longer end in a newline, reading the
    same as over HTTP+SSE, and the initialize request no longer carries an
    MCP-Protocol-Version header for a version it has not negotiated yet.
  - list_running_sessions no longer fails as a whole on a message whose
    content holds a block that is not an object ("x", 1, [1]); such a block
    is skipped, as ClaudeHistory skips it.
  - get_conversation reads the session's own file once on a cold cache, for
    its metadata and its messages together, instead of twice.
  - A call that a Streamable HTTP upstream lets through just before a start
    begins -- after a stop, or for the new session a remote-ended one needs --
    now waits for that start and goes out on its session, instead of going
    out without a session id in the middle of the handshake, where a stop
    cut it off.
  - A Streamable HTTP start that fails after the remote gave it a session, or
    that a stop calls off as its initialize goes out, now ends that session
    with a DELETE instead of leaving it open on the remote until its own
    timeout.
  - A `tool_use` block without a name shows as `[tool_use]` instead of
    warning on every read, and message content that is not an array now goes
    out as a string in `text`, `first_prompt` and `last_prompt` (a number or
    boolean as its string, an object as empty text) instead of as a JSON
    number, boolean or object.
